WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.331
Scenario: An email displays the name “Campus IT Helpdesk,”

2
00:00:03.331 --> 00:00:06.662
but the full sender address ends in an unrelated

3
00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:10.364
public email domain. It asks you to confirm your password.

4
00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:13.695
Safest response: Option A: The full sender address and

5
00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:17.026
domain, checked against a known official contact. Inspect the

6
00:00:17.026 --> 00:00:20.357
full address and verify the domain using an independently

7
00:00:20.357 --> 00:00:23.688
known directory or channel. Option B is unsafe because

8
00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:27.019
Display names are easy to choose or imitate and

9
00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:30.351
do not authenticate the sender. Option C is unsafe

10
00:00:30.351 --> 00:00:33.682
because Timing is weak evidence; malicious messages can be

11
00:00:33.682 --> 00:00:37.013
sent at any time. Option D is unsafe because

12
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:41.084
Logos can be copied and are not proof of message origin.

13
00:00:41.084 --> 00:00:44.416
In practice: Expand sender details, inspect the full address

14
00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:47.747
and domain, and contact IT through the published helpdesk

15
00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:51.078
channel if a credential request is unexpected. Key takeaway:

16
00:00:51.078 --> 00:00:54.409
A familiar name or logo is not identity proof;

17
00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:57.000
inspect and independently verify the actual address.
